The function should report whether the point lies inside the triangle or exactly on one of its three edges, and False otherwise.

The standard approach is to decide on which side of each edge the point falls, compared with the interior of the triangle. The point is inside or on an edge if it lies on the same side of each edge as the third vertex of that edge. One side of an edge is the oriented half-plane that contains the triangle, and the other side is outside. Points exactly on an edge fall on neither side, so they are reported as inside.

Here is the implementation:

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
        def cross(u, v):
            return u[0] * v[1] - u[1] * v[0]
        def same_side(p, q, a, b):
            return cross(b - a, p - a) * cross(b - a, q - a) >= 0
        return same_side(p, c, a, b) and same_side(p, a, b, c) and same_side(p, b, c, a)

The helper cross computes the z-coordinate of the cross product of two vectors, which is twice the signed area of the triangle formed by those vectors. The function same_side returns True when p and q lie on the same side of the edge from a to b, with equality allowed so that q on the edge is accepted, and False when q and p lie on opposite sides. The main function returns True only when p lies on the same side of each edge as the triangle's third vertex, and False otherwise.

The third test places the point at a vertex, and the equality in same_side accepts it, as the specification requires.

