def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])
    def dot(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[0] - o[0]) + (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[1] - o[1])
    if cross(a, b, p) != 0:
        return False
    if dot(a, b, p) < 0 or dot(b, a, p) < 0:
        return False
    return True

The function checks two conditions: whether p lies on the line through a and b, and whether it lies between them.

The cross product (a.x - p.x) * (b.y - p.y) - (a.y - p.y) * (b.x - p.x) is twice the signed area of the triangle formed by a, b and p. When it is zero, the three points are collinear, so p is on the line. The dot product (a.x - p.x) * (b.x - p.x) + (a.y - p.y) * (b.y - p.y) is negative when p lies strictly outside the segment, because the angle from p to the direction of the segment is obtuse. The second dot product with b as the origin checks the other endpoint. If either dot product is negative, p is on the line extended past an endpoint and is not part of the segment. Collinearity alone is not enough, since the collinear point (5, 5) against (0, 0) and (2, 2) is rejected by the dot product. The case where the endpoints coincide is accepted by the dot product, as both terms are zero.

assert is_point_on_segment((1, 1), (0, 0), (2, 2)) == True
assert is_point_on_segment((5, 5), (0, 0), (2, 2)) == False
assert is_point_on_segment((0, 0), (0, 0), (2, 2)) == True
The first point lies on the diagonal and between the endpoints; the second lies on the same line but outside the segment; the third is an endpoint, so it is included.

